= Mung Dhal (Mung Bean)

_Ayurvedic status: fully classical — mudga is the most-praised pulse of the Caraka and Bhāvaprakāśa traditions. Chinese: fully classical — 綠豆 is a major drug of the Bencao tradition (heat-clearing, antidote). Greco-Roman: no classical tradition (mung bean is South/East Asian, post-classical to Europe). Honest framing: the dhal (split, dehusked) is a preparation of the same drug — the saṃhitās treat it under_ mudga _with the preparation specified._

== Nomenclature & Etymology

*Latin:* _Vigna radiata_ (L.) R. Wilczek, family Fabaceae (= Leguminosae); older synonyms _Phaseolus aureus_ Roxb., _Phaseolus radiatus_ L., _Phaseolus mungo_ L. *Care:* _mungo_ is now reserved for the distinct species _Vigna mungo_ (L.) Hepper, black gram (Sanskrit _māṣa_, Hindi _urad dal_), so old _Phaseolus mungo_ references are ambiguous and frequently mis-identified in the popular literature. The genus _Vigna_ commemorates Domenico Vigna (Italian botanist, d. 1647); _radiata_ ("radiating, rayed") refers to the seed-scar / hilum. The drug is the seed; *dhal* / _dāl_ specifically denotes the split, dehusked form.#footnote[Species identity and synonymy: _Flora of China_, vol. 10 (Fabaceae); _The Plant List_ / WFO; for the _Phaseolus mungo_ ambiguity and its consequences for materia-medica identifications, see Verdcourt's African Fabaceae treatments and the older standard sources. The "_radiata_" epithet refers to the hilum, per standard botanical descriptions.]

*English:* _Mung bean_ (also _moong_, _mungo_, _green gram_), from Hindi _मूँग_ (_mūm̐g_), from Sanskrit _mudga_; the older spelling _mungo_ generated the species-name confusion noted above. _Dhal_ / _dāl_ / _daal_ — the split, dehusked-pulse preparation — from Sanskrit/Pali √_dal-_ "to split, divide" (cf. Hindi _dālnā_). In English, "mung dal" / "moong dal" almost always denotes the *yellow split dehusked* form; the whole green bean is normally called "mung bean" or "green gram."#footnote[_Oxford English Dictionary_ (s.v. "mung, n.", "dhal/dal/daal, n."); Monier-Williams, _A Sanskrit–English Dictionary_ (s.v. √_dal_, _mudga_). The yellow-split / green-whole distinction follows standard South Asian culinary usage.]

*Chinese:* 綠豆 (lǜdòu), lit. "green bean" — 綠 (lǜ, "green") + 豆 (dòu, "bean/pulse"). The graph 豆 originally depicted a sacrificial vessel and was extended to "bean/pulse." Distinct named forms in the Chinese materia medica:
- *綠豆 (lǜdòu)* — the whole green bean: the principal drug.
- *綠豆皮 / 綠豆衣 (lǜdòupí / lǜdòuyī, "mung skin/coat")* — the seed-coat alone, used as a distinct lighter heat-clearing drug.
- *綠豆芽 (lǜdòuyá)* — mung bean sprouts (the culinary "bean sprouts").
- *綠豆瓣 (lǜdòubàn, "mung petals/splits")* / *脫皮綠豆 (tuōpí lǜdòu, "dehulled mung bean")* — the dehusked split form, equivalent to Indian mung dal.#footnote[Character glosses: _Hanyu Da Cidian_ (漢語大詞典); the 綠豆 / 綠豆皮 / 綠豆芽 distinctions follow the standard materia medica — see Li Shizhen, _Bencao Gangmu_ (本草綱目), 1578, grain/pulse section, and Bensky, Clavey & Stöger, _Chinese Herbal Medicine: Materia Medica_, 3rd ed. (Eastland Press, 2004).]

*Sanskrit:* मुद्ग (_mudga_) — the classical name; etymology obscure (possibly a Dravidian or Munda substrate term given the South Asian origin of the crop). Monier-Williams: "_mudga_, the (Indian) kidney-bean." Nighaṇṭu synonyms: हरितमुद्ग (_harita-mudga_, "green mudga" — the standard culinary form), वर्णमुद्ग, मगध (_magadha_), विरतण्डुल; the dehusked split form is referred to in later Sanskrit as मुद्गदाल (_mudga-dāla_) or specified by preparation in the saṃhitās (e.g. _mudga-yūṣa_ as the soup of cooked split mung, often dehusked). The well-known classical preparations _mudga-yūṣa_ and the rice-and-mung _kṛsara_ / _khichari_ tradition are part of the same lexical family.#footnote[Monier-Williams, _A Sanskrit–English Dictionary_ (s.v. _mudga_); synonym lists from Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu, _Dhānya-varga_ (_śimbi-dhānya_ subsection), and Dhanvantari Nighaṇṭu. The etymology is uncertain and is flagged as such.]

== Classical Chinese Medicine (中醫) View

綠豆 is a fully canonical drug, treated since at least the _Kaibao Bencao_ (973 CE) and given a long entry in _Bencao Gangmu_; it is the archetypal *summer-heat* drug and a classical *antidote*.#footnote[綠豆 entry: Li Shizhen, _Bencao Gangmu_ (本草綱目), 1578, grain/pulse section; earlier in _Kaibao Bencao_ (開寶本草, 973 CE). English: Unschuld, _Ben Cao Gang Mu_ (Univ. of California Press, 2021); Bensky et al., _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed.]

*Property (性 xìng / 氣 qì):* Cold (寒) (some texts: cool, 涼 — the whole bean is cold, the bean-coat 綠豆皮 cool to cold and lighter, the sprouts cool).

*Flavor (味 wèi):* Sweet (甘 gān).

*Channels entered (歸經 guījīng):* Heart (心), Stomach (胃); some sources add Liver (肝).#footnote[Property, flavor and channels: _Bencao Gangmu_ 綠豆 entry; Bensky et al., _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed. Variant 寒/涼 attributions appear across the literature and are flagged.]

*Actions & indications:*
- 清熱解毒 — clears heat and resolves toxicity (the signature action). Used for heat-toxin sores and carbuncles, and as the classical *antidote of first resort* in food and drug poisoning (including arsenic and certain plant alkaloid poisonings, by classical attribution).
- 消暑 — resolves summer-heat (the principal use, embodied in the famous summer drink 綠豆湯).
- 利水 — promotes urination, drains water (helpful in summer-heat with thirst, urinary scantiness, and damp-heat skin disease).
- 止渴 — quenches thirst; for _xiāokě_-pattern thirst with heat signs.
- *綠豆皮 / 綠豆衣 specifically:* lighter, clears the surface (clearing summer-heat from the eyes and head, dispersing eruptions); used in eye and skin work.#footnote[Action list and the antidote use: _Bencao Gangmu_ 綠豆 entry (Li Shizhen records the antidotal application for both food and drug toxicity); systematized in Bensky et al., _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed. The 綠豆甘草湯 antidote pairing is a standard heat-toxicity formula; see Bensky & Barolet, _Formulas & Strategies_, 2nd ed.]

*Cautions:* Cold nature — contraindicated in Spleen-and-Stomach vacuity-cold, with loose stools or chronic diarrhea; unsuitable in cold-pattern menstrual disorders. The classical caution _藥忌綠豆_ — "(certain) medicinals are antagonized by 綠豆" — captures its detoxifying action used the other way around: 綠豆 *neutralizes* the action of warm/hot herbal medicinals (notably _wū tóu_ 烏頭 / aconite, and warming tonics generally), so it is classically *not* taken during such a course of treatment.#footnote[The 藥忌綠豆 caution: _Bencao Gangmu_ 綠豆 entry; standard pharmacy lore — mung-bean's antidotal property cuts both ways. Cf. Bensky et al., _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed.]

== Ayurvedic Dravyaguna (द्रव्यगुण)

_Mudga_ is the most-praised pulse in the Caraka tradition — _śreṣṭha_ ("best") among the _śimbi-dhānya_ (legumes) — and is the canonical convalescent food (as _mudga-yūṣa_) across the saṃhitās.#footnote[_Mudga_ as best of pulses: Caraka Saṃhitā, Sūtrasthāna 27 (_annapānavidhi_, _śimbi-varga_); Suśruta Saṃhitā, Sūtrasthāna 46 (_anna-pāna-vidhi_); Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu, _Dhānya-varga_, _mudga_ entry (Chunekar & Pandey ed., Chaukhamba). Translations: Sharma & Dash, _Caraka Saṃhitā_ (Chowkhamba); Murthy, _Suśruta Saṃhitā_ / _Bhāvaprakāśa_ (Chaukhamba).]

*Rasa (taste):* Madhura (मधुर, sweet), Kaṣāya (कषाय, astringent).

*Guṇa (qualities):* Laghu (लघु, light) — notably so, as pulses go — and Rūkṣa (रूक्ष, dry). The lightness despite the legume class is a defining note and underwrites the convalescent use.

*Vīrya (potency):* Śīta (शीत, cooling).

*Vipāka (post-digestive taste):* Kaṭu (कटु, pungent).#footnote[Rasa–guṇa–vīrya–vipāka of mudga: Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu, _Dhānya-varga_; Caraka Saṃhitā, Sūtrasthāna 27. The _kaṭu_ vipāka is regular for the pulse class but is sometimes given as _madhura_ in particular preparations — flagged.]

*Prabhāva (specific action):* The classical texts do not isolate a single named prabhāva for mudga distinct from its rasa-guṇa-vīrya-vipāka — its principal actions (Pitta-and-Kapha-pacifying, light, easy-to-digest, convalescent-supportive) flow from the sweet-astringent / laghu-rūkṣa / cooling / pungent-vipāka profile. The notable *exception within the pulse class* — that mudga, unlike most other _śimbi-dhānya_, is light enough to be tridoṣa-friendly in moderation and _pathya_ (wholesome) in nearly all disease states — is sometimes read by commentators as prabhāva-level (in the same sense milk's _ojas-vardhana_ status is). Flagged as commentarial/interpretive rather than as a stated canonical prabhāva.#footnote[On prabhāva as a category: Caraka Saṃhitā, Sūtrasthāna 26.67–72. The "best of pulses" status of mudga is canonical (Caraka, Sūtrasthāna 27); the *prabhāva-level* reading of that lightness is interpretive — cf. P. V. Sharma, _Dravyaguṇa-vijñāna_, vol. II, _mudga_; explicitly flagged as such.]

*Doṣa effect:* Pacifies Pitta and Kapha (pitta-kapha-śāmaka); mildly Vāta-aggravating if eaten without proper unctuous preparation (with ghee, oil, asafoetida — i.e. the standard South Asian dhal seasoning that classically counteracts the Vāta tendency). Pacifies all three doṣas in moderate, well-prepared use.#footnote[Doṣa attribution: Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu, _Dhānya-varga_; Caraka Saṃhitā, Sūtrasthāna 27. The classical Vāta-corrective seasoning (ghee + asafoetida + cumin + ginger) is itself doctrinal — see Caraka's preparation notes and Sharangadhara Saṃhitā for _yūṣa_ preparation.]

*Karma (actions):*
- _Hṛdya_ — cardiotonic / wholesome to the heart.
- _Grāhī_ — binding (helpful in diarrhea and excess intestinal fluidity).
- _Cakṣuṣya_ — good for the eyes.
- _Balya_, _laghu-bṛṃhaṇa_ — mildly strengthening in a light register (suitable for the convalescent).
- _Pathya_, _nitya-sevanīya_ — wholesome, suitable for daily use.
- Indicated for: _jvara_ (fever, in convalescence), _atisāra_ (diarrhea), _kāsa_ and _śvāsa_ (cough/dyspnea, supportively), _kuṣṭha_ (skin diseases — supportive), _amlapitta_ (hyperacidity), _raktapitta_ (bleeding/Pitta-blood disorders), and as the staple food during fever recovery, post-partum, and in pañcakarma (post-_vamana_, post-_virecana_ light diet).
- _Mudga-yūṣa_ (mung soup) is the canonical convalescent food — the classical preparation: split, dehusked mung boiled with water to a thin soup, with ginger, asafoetida, and a touch of ghee/salt, depending on the doṣa context.#footnote[Karma compiled from Caraka Saṃhitā, Sūtrasthāna 27, and Cikitsāsthāna chapters on _jvara_ (3), _atisāra_ (19), and _raktapitta_ (4); Suśruta Saṃhitā, Sūtrasthāna 46; Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu, _Dhānya-varga_; standard convalescent-diet usage is consistently described from Caraka onward. _Mudga-yūṣa_ preparation: Caraka Saṃhitā and Sharangadhara Saṃhitā (preparation chapters); cf. Murthy translations (Chaukhamba).]

=== Viruddha (विरुद्ध — incompatibilities)

_(tagged by source tier — S = core saṃhitā, N = later nighaṇṭu, F = folk/regional, M = modern/pharmacological)_

Mudga's profile is the *least* viruddha-laden of major foods — its classical reputation rests precisely on being broadly compatible.

- *(S) Conditional (doṣa) caution:* mildly Vāta-aggravating dry/light qualities should be corrected with proper unctuous seasoning (ghee, oil, asafoetida); plain dry preparation is unsuitable in pronounced Vāta vacuity. (Not a saṃyoga-viruddha but an _avasthā/saṃskāra_ rule.)
- *(N) Few named food-pair viruddhas:* nighaṇṭu literature does not record an elaborate list of saṃyoga-incompatibilities for mudga; on the contrary, _mudga_ + rice (the _kṛsara_/khichari combination) is repeatedly praised.
- *(F) Folk regional:* occasional cautions about heavy mudga + curd (dadhi) combinations in some regional sources, on the general saṃskāra principle.
- *(M) Modern / pharmacological:*
  - *Cross-system (CCM) caution:* concurrent use with warming herbal tonics (notably aconite-based formulas) — by the Chinese 藥忌綠豆 principle, mung bean *antidotes* / weakens them. A real cross-system interaction.
  - Mung-bean *allergy* exists but is uncommon globally; documented mostly in East Asian populations as cross-reactivity with other legumes.
  - *FODMAP / oligosaccharide* (raffinose, stachyose) load — produces gas/bloating in sensitive guts; the classical Vāta caution is the dietetic restatement of this.#footnote[The classical Vāta caution and the modern FODMAP / raffinose-stachyose explanation map cleanly onto each other; this is one of the cleaner cross-system convergences. The 藥忌綠豆 cross-system interaction is real and is flagged. Mung-bean allergy: dermatology / allergy literature — verify against current allergen-cross-reactivity reviews.]

== Traditional Arabic & Islamic Medicine (Ṭibb al-ʿArabī wa-l-Islāmī / Unani)

_Mung bean entered Tibb via Persian-Indian transmission and is canonical in Unani as_ māsh / māsh muqashshar _("split / dehusked māsh"). See_ tibb-al-arabi.typ.

*Arabic / Persian:* ماش (_māsh_) — from Persian (same word); Avicenna treats it. Urdu/Hakeemi: ماش / مونگ (_māsh / mūng_, the Hindi-Urdu term).

*Mizāj:* *Cold and Dry in the 1st degree* (Ibn Sīnā, *al-Qānūn* II, _māsh_). Converges with the Ayurvedic śīta vīrya. The Chinese 寒/涼 also converges — clean tri-system convergence.

*Af'āl:* *Mughadhdhī yasīr* (lightly nourishing — the canonical "lightest pulse" register, paralleling the Ayurvedic _laghu_ classification); *muqawwī al-mi'da*; *qābiḍ* (binding, in some preparations); *mufariḥ* (mood-elevating, modest); *mukhrij li-l-balgham al-yābis*; *muḥaffif li-l-ḥummā* (antipyretic — the famous Tibb convalescent-fever soup); *mudirr li-l-bawl* (mild diuretic).

*Istemālāt:* convalescence (the principal indication; mung soup is the Tibb convalescent-from-fever food par excellence, paralleling the Ayurvedic mudga-yūṣa exactly); fever recovery; gastric weakness; mild diarrhea (the binding action); hot-pattern dyspepsia; reduces toxicity.

*Mawāne':* heavy cold-pattern; severe Vāta-equivalent dryness without unctuous correction.

*Muṣliḥ:* ghee (*samn*) + cumin + ginger + asafoetida (the canonical Tibb pulse-correction seasoning, *identical to the Ayurvedic correction*).

*Badal:* red lentils (*ʿadas*); other small pulses.

*Tibb formulas:* *Māsh-yūsh / Māsh-yūṣ* — mung soup, the convalescent food (the very word _yūṣ_ is a direct Sanskrit-Persian-Arabic loan from Ayurvedic _yūṣa_, attesting the Indian origin); *Khichṛī* — the rice-and-mung dish adopted into Mughal Unani as a balanced base food; *Sufūf-i Māsh* (mung-flour powder for skin / cosmetic use, paralleling the South Asian _ubṭan_ tradition).

*Source attestation:* Ibn Sīnā, *al-Qānūn* II, _māsh_; Ibn al-Bayṭār, *al-Jāmiʿ*; *Khazāyin al-Adwiya*.#footnote[Tibb māsh: Avicennan classical reading; the tri-system Ayurvedic-Tibb-Chinese convergence on cooling-light-easy-digestion is exact.]

== Classical Formulas & Preparations

=== Chinese medicine (中醫)

- *綠豆湯 (Lǜdòu Tāng, "Mung Bean Decoction/Soup")* — not a "formula" in the strict prescription sense but a foundational dietetic preparation: mung beans cooked to a thin sweet soup (often with a touch of sugar in modern practice) as the standard summer-heat / dehydration remedy. The canonical 中醫 dietetic drink.
- *綠豆甘草湯 (Lǜdòu Gāncǎo Tāng, "Mung Bean and Licorice Decoction")* — mung bean + raw licorice; the classical antidote pairing for general food and drug poisoning. Both ingredients clear heat-toxin and harmonize.
- *綠豆百合粥 (Lǜdòu Bǎihé Zhōu, "Mung Bean and Lily-bulb Porridge")* — mung + Lilium bulbs in congee form, for restless heat with insomnia; a dietetic preparation.
- *綠豆海帶湯 (Lǜdòu Hǎidài Tāng)* — mung bean with kelp, for damp-heat skin eruptions (modern dietetic extension of the classical heat-clearing principle).
- *Topical uses:* mung-bean powder pastes for heat-toxin skin sores, burns, prickly heat; classical and continuing.
- *Sprouts (綠豆芽)* are treated as a separate item: cool, sweet, light; used as a heat-clearing vegetable in summer dietetics. Roughly 9–30 g of dried mung beans is a typical decoction dose; far larger amounts are used as food.#footnote[Formula and dietetic references: _Bencao Gangmu_ 綠豆 entry; Sun Simiao, _Qianjin Yaofang / Shizhi_ (千金食治); modern TCM dietetics handbooks (e.g. Kastner, _Chinese Nutrition Therapy_). 綠豆甘草湯 antidote pairing: standard heat-toxicity therapy, cf. Bensky & Barolet, _Formulas & Strategies_, 2nd ed.]

=== Ayurveda

- *Mudga-yūṣa (मुद्गयूष, "mung soup")* — the canonical Ayurvedic convalescent food. Split, dehusked mudga boiled thin with water and a calibrated seasoning (ghee, asafoetida, ginger, salt, sometimes cumin / coriander / black pepper). The standard food for the recovering patient, the post-pañcakarma patient, and the febrile patient as the fever resolves. Repeatedly recommended in Caraka Cikitsāsthāna.
- *Kṛsara (कृसर) / Khichari (खिचड़ी)* — the classical mudga-and-rice combination cooked to a soft, easily-digested whole. The traditional _saṃskāra_ point: cooking mudga with rice corrects the Vāta-aggravating dryness of mudga and the cloying heaviness of rice — each balances the other. Khichari is also the staple food of _pañcakarma_ (the _saṃsarjana-krama_ post-purgation diet) and of fasting/ritual cleansing.
- *Mudga-pāyasa* — sweet milk-mudga preparation; nourishing in Pitta-vacuity convalescence.
- *Pañca-yūṣa* — the "five soups," a Caraka classification of dietetic soups for various doṣa/disease states; mudga-yūṣa is the most central member.
- *In compound formulas:* mudga appears in several classical compound powders/decoctions as a base or supportive ingredient; the bulk of its tradition is dietetic-medical rather than pharmaceutical-compound.#footnote[Mudga-yūṣa, kṛsara, pañca-yūṣa: Caraka Saṃhitā, Sūtrasthāna 27, and across Cikitsāsthāna; Sharangadhara Saṃhitā for preparation methods of _yūṣa_/_kṛsara_; standard convalescent-diet usage is universal in the Ayurvedic tradition. Translations: Sharma & Dash; Murthy. The _saṃskāra_-corrective reading of mudga + rice in khichari is the standard dravyaguna explanation, well attested in commentary.]

=== Western / Greco-Roman and later European medicine

No classical European tradition for mung bean — the species reached Europe only in the modern era, and was virtually unknown to Greco-Roman, medieval, and early-modern European pharmacy. The various ancient European pulses (_Vicia faba_ broad bean, _Lens culinaris_ lentil, _Cicer arietinum_ chickpea, _Pisum sativum_ pea, _Lupinus albus_ lupine) have their own Dioscoridean / Galenic / Hildegardian traditions, but mung bean is *not* among them, and any modern Western herbal/dietetic claim about mung bean is — honestly — an import from the Asian traditions, not a Western lineage.#footnote[The South/East Asian origin and post-classical European introduction of _V. radiata_ is botanical/agronomic fact. The mainstream Greco-Roman pulse entries are in Dioscorides, _De Materia Medica_ II.105 (lentil), II.106 (broad bean), II.110 (chickpea), II.111 (lupine) — Beck translation; Galen, _On the Properties of Foodstuffs_, Book I. None of these is _Vigna radiata_. The honest "no Western tradition" note is per the section rule in the project guide.]

== Modern Nutrition & Pharmacology

Composition, dehulled split mung dhal, approximate values per 100 g raw — *verify against USDA FoodData Central* (current edition).#footnote[Composition figures are approximate; verify against USDA FoodData Central (https://fdc.nal.usda.gov), current edition. The whole green mung bean and the dehulled yellow split forms have very similar nutrient profiles, with somewhat lower fiber in the dehulled form.]

- Energy ~340 kcal; Water ~9 g
- Protein ~24 g (one of the highest among edible pulses, with a comparatively favorable amino acid profile — adequate lysine, modest methionine; well complemented by cereal grains in combination)
- Fat ~1.2 g (very low)
- Carbohydrate ~63 g; Dietary fiber ~16 g (whole bean) / ~8 g (dehulled split)
- Folate ~625 µg (one of the richest dietary sources by mass)
- Magnesium ~190 mg; Potassium ~1250 mg; Manganese, copper, phosphorus
- Iron ~7 mg (non-heme; bioavailability moderate, improved by vitamin-C co-ingestion); Zinc ~2.7 mg
- Phytic acid (whole bean; reduced by sprouting and soaking)

*Pharmacology / physiology of note:*
- *Easy digestibility relative to other pulses:* mung bean has *lower* total oligosaccharides (raffinose, stachyose, verbascose) than soy and many other pulses, and dehulling further reduces them — the modern correlate of the classical _laghu_ (light) classification.
- *Trypsin inhibitors and phytohaemagglutinins:* present but modest, and substantially destroyed by ordinary cooking; the bean is *safe when properly cooked*, in line with all classical preparations.
- *Phenolic / antioxidant content* — vitexin and isovitexin are notable flavone C-glycosides in the seed coat (hence the lighter, surface-clearing action ascribed to 綠豆皮 in TCM).
- *Antidote / hepatoprotective action* — animal data support some hepatoprotective activity and protection against certain organophosphate / heavy-metal toxicities for mung-bean preparations; the 綠豆 antidote tradition has a modest pharmacological correlate. Clinical evidence in humans is preliminary.
- *Sprouted mung bean:* gains vitamin C, loses phytate; nutritional profile shifts toward a leafy-vegetable register — consistent with the 綠豆芽 classification in Chinese dietetics.
- *Allergy:* mung-bean IgE allergy is uncommon globally but documented, with cross-reactivity to other legumes; clinically significant chiefly in East Asian populations.#footnote[Oligosaccharide content of mung vs. other pulses: published pulse-chemistry literature (verify against current reviews). Trypsin-inhibitor / phytohaemagglutinin profile and cooking-inactivation: standard food-toxicology data. Vitexin/isovitexin in mung seed coat: phytochemistry reviews. Hepatoprotective/antitoxin animal data: preclinical literature; clinical evidence preliminary — verify against current systematic reviews. Allergy data: regional allergology literature.]

*Safe daily dosage / upper limit:* No toxicological upper limit — mung dhal is a whole food and one of the most reliably tolerated. Practical considerations rather than a toxic ceiling:
- *As food:* a routine cooked-dhal serving (~30–50 g raw, ~150–200 g cooked) raises no specific safety question; mung dhal is appropriate for daily / frequent consumption.
- *As medicinal (Chinese summer-heat / antidote drink):* 9–30 g dried beans in decoction is the classical dose range; therapeutic preparations are *short-term* — long high-dose use of mung in concert with warming herbal medicine would antagonize the latter (the 藥忌綠豆 caution).
- *Risk groups:* legume allergy; concurrent course of warming Chinese herbal medicine (timing precaution, not absolute); FODMAP-sensitive IBS (case-by-case — dehulled split mung tolerates better than whole).
- *Cooking matters:* like all pulses, must be cooked thoroughly to inactivate residual lectins / inhibitors. Sprouting and proper cooking are not optional dietary preferences but classical / pharmacological requirements.#footnote[These are reference thresholds and practical figures, not personalized medical advice. Cooking-inactivation of lectins / inhibitors is the universal pulse rule; the 藥忌綠豆 timing caution is a real cross-system pharmacological point. For clinical use (e.g. in mung-rich convalescent or detox protocols), consult a qualified clinician familiar with the tradition involved.]

